// =================================================================================================
// SEVERE weathering for the CLASSIC motif's SURFACE ENTRANCE, and nothing else. Named by
// template_pool/entrance/classic/surface_entrance.json (Gottsch, 2026-08-29).
//
// WHY THE ENTRANCE GETS ITS OWN LIST. It is the one piece of a dungeon that stands in the open, in
// daylight, exposed to weather for however long the fiction says -- so it should be visibly more
// ruined than the corridors below it, which have been sealed underground. Everything under the
// entrance keeps classic_weathering.json's rates.
//
// IT IS A COPY, NOT AN EXTENSION, and it has to be: a pool element names exactly ONE
// processor_list, so there is no mechanism for composing this with the motif's. The same constraint
// classic_mud_weathering.json's header explains, and the same consequence -- everything below that
// is NOT about severity is duplicated from classic_weathering.json and has to be kept in step by
// hand. #47 rewrites all three.
//
// THE RATES ARE NO LONGER CLASSIC'S (2026-09-18). The fork finally got the tuning pass it was made
// for: every stage of every chain in this file, and every probability in the vanilla rule
// processors, is 0.5. That is the number Mark asked for and it is meant to be read as one number
// rather than as a per-block judgement -- retune the whole file by changing them together, not by
// picking at individual chains.
//
// 0.5 EVERYWHERE WAS NOT A RUIN, and the reason is the single most useful thing in this header.
// A conditional chain thins fast -- a 4-stage chain at 0.5 reaches its last stage 6.25% of the time,
// not 50% -- and worse, every stage that is not air produces a FULL CUBE. Mossy, cracked, andesite:
// half a wall being mossy is still a wall. The first pass at 0.5 left a stone brick wall ~4.7% air
// and it read, correctly, as a well-kept building with moss on it (Mark, in game, 2026-09-18).
//
// SO THE SHAPE OF THE FILE CHANGED, not just its numbers. Three edits made it a husk, and they are
// listed in the order of how much each one matters:
//
//   1. COLLAPSE BY HEIGHT, the last section of the surface_aging rules -- masonry goes to air at
//      0.6 in the top third of the piece, 0.25 in the middle and 0.05 at the footings. This is
//      the ruin, and its own comment explains why the SPREAD matters more than the average. It
//      replaced a flat 0.25 knockout on 2026-09-18, which brought the building down evenly and
//      left courses hanging in the sky.
//   2. mossy_stone_bricks and cracked_stone_bricks crumble onward instead of terminating, so the
//      0.5 pass feeds the debris route rather than parking blocks in an "aged" dead end.
//   3. Debris (rubble / mossy_rubble / gravel) blows away on the SAME gradient, steeper: 0.85 at
//      the crown down to 0.15 at the base. It was a flat 0.8 in a processor of its own, which
//      quietly undid the base band -- see the debris note in the collapse section. Every chain in
//      the aging processor whose LAST stage is air ends at 0.8 rather than 0.5.
//
// The material passes leave a stone brick wall block roughly 33% mossy, 17% cracked, 17% cobble
// or mossy cobble and 12% untouched, with the rest debris; collapse then takes its share of ALL
// of that by height. dungeons2:support_sweep runs last and drops what the holes left floating --
// which, now that the top third is mostly gone, is a collapsed upper storey rather than the
// perforated one the flat rate produced.
//
// THREE RATES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT 0.5, each for a gameplay reason rather than a visual one:
//
//   * minecraft:ladder -- aged, but at the lowest rates in the file (0.4 / 0.2 / 0.08 by band),
//     because a pristine ladder up the middle of a collapsed building reads as a bug. It is
//     also `nonstructural` on the sweep, so what survives of it holds nothing up.
//   * minecraft:chain / dungeonblocks:swinging_chain -- held at 0.1. dungeons2:hanging_sweep drops
//     everything below a broken link, so the rate is per RUN, not per block: at 0.5 a link,
//     essentially no lantern run in the shaft survives and the descent goes dark.
//   * dungeonblocks:dungeon_lantern -- held at 0.15, same reason. It is the light the chain carries.
//
// Raise the FIRST stage of a chain to make more of the building decay at all, and the LAST to trade
// crumbled stone for open holes.
//
// AND THAT IS WHAT dungeons2:support_sweep AT THE BOTTOM IS FOR. Severe decay eats blocks out of
// the middle of a wall, and what was resting on them does not fall -- a template writes what it is
// told, so you get masonry floating over a gap, which reads as a bug rather than as a ruin. The
// sweep drops anything left with no path to the ground. It MUST be the last entry: it can only
// judge what the passes above it finally left behind.
// =================================================================================================
//
// PROBABILITIES ARE CONDITIONAL, NOT ABSOLUTE -- in both processors below.
//
//   * Down a chain ("output_blocks"): a stage only rolls if the stage before it hit.
//   * Across chains: when one source block has several rules, they are alternatives
//     tried in order, and a later one only rolls if the earlier ones missed.
//
// THE FORK FORMULA. To make the k-th rule listed on one source block take share s_k
// of that block, author:
//
//     p_k = s_k / (1 - (s_1 + s_2 + ... + s_k-1))
//
// i.e. divide the share you want by the fraction still unclaimed. Only the FIRST
// rule's authored number equals its share; nothing shields it.
//
// So two alternative chains that should each fire 30% of the time are authored
// 0.3 and 0.43 (0.3 / 0.7), not 0.3 and 0.3. A third at 20% is then 0.5. An even
// 15/15 split is 0.15 and 0.1765 (0.15 / 0.85) -- measured on the mud band's floor
// pair, which lands at 11.25% each once each route's own stage 2 has thinned it.
//
// To read an existing file the other way: s_k = p_k * (1-p_1) * ... * (1-p_k-1).
//
// A FORK IS TWO RULES ON ONE SOURCE BLOCK, not two entries in one rule's
// "output_blocks" -- that is a CHAIN, where stage 2 only rolls if stage 1 hit and
// REPLACES what stage 1 produced rather than being an alternative to it.
//
// Some splits are arithmetically impossible: if the shares before rule k already
// claim most of the block, the required p_k exceeds 1. You cannot have 60% and 50%.
//
// Rules for DIFFERENT source blocks never interfere.
//
// See dungeons2-forge-1.20.1-ProcessorManual.md for the whole pipeline.
//
// Only processors vetted for a procedural piece belong here -- see PieceProcessors. This list is
// named by an authored prefab and by nothing procedural, but the sweep in
// StratumWeatheringListTest holds every *_weathering.json to the same bar, deliberately: a file
// that is prefab-only today is one motif away from not being.
{
  "processors": [
    {
      // dungeons2:surface_aging -- for SHAPED blocks (stairs, slabs, walls). Unlike a vanilla
      // rule, it carries the source block's properties (facing/half/shape/waterlogged)
      // onto the replacement, and supports multi-stage decay chains.
      // "agings" caps how many stages of a chain may apply.
      //
      // WAS dungeons2:aging until 2026-09-18. It was swapped for surface_aging to get the
      // "elevation" gate, which the COLLAPSE section at the bottom of these rules needs; every
      // rule above that section names no surface and no band, and surface_aging is a strict
      // superset of aging, so none of them changed meaning in the swap.
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:surface_aging",
      "agings": 4,
      "rules": [
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_brick_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_brick_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:polished_andesite_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:andesite_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_stone_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_stone_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:spruce_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_wall",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone_wall", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_slab",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_brick_slab", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_slab", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:spruce_slab",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_wall",
          "output_blocks": [
            {
              "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_brick_wall",
              "probability": 0.5
            },
            {
              "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_wall",
              "probability": 0.5
            },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:polished_andesite_crown_molding_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:polished_andesite_crown_molding_block", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_cobblestone_crown_molding_block", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:polished_andesite_facade_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:polished_andesite_facade_block", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_cobblestone_facade_block", "probability": 0.5 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },

        // ===================================================================================
        // TIMBER (Backlog #37, added 2026-08-13). Until now this file had 110 rules and not
        // one touched wood, so the joisted_hall's spruce hung factory-fresh in a room of
        // cracked, mossy, crumbling brick -- in the most visible position in the room.
        //
        // AIR IS SAFE HERE IN A WAY IT IS NOT ELSEWHERE, and that is still worth knowing even
        // though the deep-decay ban was lifted on 2026-08-25. Timber is the one material in this
        // dungeon that already has what #15 asks for: spruce_log and the spruce corbel are used
        // for beams and brackets and NOTHING else, and a beam hangs BELOW the ceiling slab
        // rather than being part of it, so a decayed beam leaves a gap in the run with the shell
        // intact. These rates were therefore never a compromise -- unlike a stone chain's air,
        // which is now allowed but does breach. Move spruce_log into a wall or ceiling and that
        // distinction is lost.
        //
        // ONE STAGE, AND STRIPPED IS NOT IT (revised 2026-08-14, after Mark walked it in game).
        // The original chain went spruce_log -> stripped -> air, which put ~30% of beams in a
        // stripped state. It does not read as age: stripped spruce is a clean, pale, evenly
        // planed block, so a stripped beam beside an untouched one reads as two DIFFERENT
        // materials chosen by an author rather than as one material that weathered. The gap
        // reads correctly, so the chain now goes straight to it.
        //
        // dungeonblocks ships NO aged timber -- 694 blocks, and the only wood variants are
        // stripped_* and the species families. So "stripped" and "gone" were the entire
        // vocabulary available, and dropping the first leaves exactly one stage. A genuinely
        // weathered log block is what it would take to have a middle state here at all.
        //
        // BOTH RULES MUST BE dungeons2:aging, not minecraft:rule. A log carries `axis` and
        // the corbel carries `facing`, and a vanilla rule drops properties: JoistSurface-
        // PatternProvider turns each log to lie ALONG its run, so a vanilla rule would silently
        // stand every weathered beam on end. That is moot while the only output is air, but it
        // stops being moot the moment anyone adds a real intermediate block back.
        //
        // COMPOSED RATES. Single stage now, so the rate IS the outcome -- no compounding, and
        // these chains are each block's only rule so no conditional discount applies either:
        //
        //     spruce_log     ->  15% gone   (85% untouched)
        //     spruce corbel  ->  10% gone   (90% untouched)
        //
        // The rates were RAISED from the old chain's 5.3% / 3.5% break-through, deliberately.
        // Those were second-stage figures sitting behind a 35% first stage that was carrying
        // the visible weathering; with stripped gone, holding them would have left timber ~95%
        // factory-fresh, which is the state #37 was raised to fix.
        //
        // The corbel stays below the beam for the original reason: a bracket is a single cell
        // and reads as missing, where a beam is a run and reads as broken. AgingChainRatesTest
        // asserts both, so retune there too.
        //
        // NOT AGED, deliberately:
        //   * the dungeon doors (spruce/dark_oak) -- dungeonblocks ships no aged form of one,
        //     and "sometimes there is no door" is ALREADY a feature (the motif's door
        //     probability, ~1/3 of openings). Ageing them to air would double-count it.
        //   * the stripped variants, in EITHER role. They are no longer produced as a stage,
        //     and they must not become sources: stripped_spruce_log appears in 8 authored
        //     templates where the author chose it, so a rule keyed on it would age the
        //     author's own choice. (While it WAS a stage, that was safe for a reason worth
        //     keeping: the processor looks a rule up by the input block once and never
        //     re-resolves what it just produced, so producing a block never ages it.)
        // ===================================================================================
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:spruce_log",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:spruce_planks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:spruce_door",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:spruce_trapdoor",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:spruce_corbel_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:iron_bars",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        // The sconce is entrance_exit_1's wall light. It is a single decorative cell holding a
        // torch, not a structural block and not the only light on the piece, so it takes the file's
        // rate with no exception argued for it.
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:torch_sconce_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.8 }
          ]
        },
        // ===================================================================================
        // COLLAPSE, BY HEIGHT (2026-09-18). This is what makes the entrance read as a RUIN rather
        // than as a building with damage sprayed evenly over it.
        //
        // THE PROBLEM IT FIXES, from three screenshots Mark took of the flat 0.25 knockout it
        // replaces: the building came down, but it came down UNIFORMLY, so what was left was
        // isolated courses hanging in the sky and single blocks floating beside a column. That is
        // not support_sweep failing -- the sweep asks whether a block connects in any of six
        // directions to something grounded, deliberately, so a lintel and a corbel survive, and a
        // top course still touching the one surviving column is grounded by that test and stays.
        // A flat rate GUARANTEES those fragments, because it thins every height equally and the
        // last column standing keeps whatever is bolted to it.
        //
        // A real ruin loses its top first. Weather, gravity and anything that knocked it down all
        // work from above, and the footings are the last thing standing. So: thirds of the piece's
        // own height, and the rate falls with them.
        //
        //     crown   0.6    the top third goes almost entirely
        //     middle  0.25   broken but standing
        //     base    0.05   footings, doorsills, the outline of the building on the ground
        //
        // KEYED ON THE DECAYED IDS, NOT JUST THE AUTHORED ONES, and this is the part that is easy
        // to get wrong. A banded rule has to run in the whole-piece phase, and EVERY processBlock
        // pass in a list runs before ANY finalize pass -- so by the time this section sees the
        // building, the minecraft:rule processors below have already turned most of its stone
        // mossy, cracked, cobbled or andesite. Keying only on minecraft:stone_bricks would collapse
        // the handful of blocks that happened to survive all of them. Every id the cascade can
        // produce is listed instead.
        //
        // TO TUNE: these three numbers are the ruin, and their SPREAD matters more than their
        // average -- flattening them back towards each other is what produced the screenshots.
        // ===================================================================================
        // CROWN -- 0.6
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "elevation": "crown",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.6 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "elevation": "crown",
          "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.6 }
          ]
